@node Overview
@chapter Overview

MVP: M0–M6 — Tharsis v0.1, a playable Linux single-player port

@strong{Version:} @value{VERSION}.

@strong{Status: proposed requirements, not an implementation report.}
@file{PRD-Rust-GPU.texi} is the authoritative source for this proposed PRD;
@file{PRD-Rust-GPU.md} and @file{PRD-Rust-GPU.html} are generated reading
copies. The older @file{PRD.md} is a historical input, not a second active
requirements baseline. It remains unchanged. A future approved baseline
shall record any changes made during maintainer review.

Tracking items in this revision use @code{R-201} through @code{R-251}.
Their existing @code{Q-001} through @code{Q-051} identifiers remain permanent
aliases: add 200 to the Q number to obtain its R identifier. The older
@file{PRD.md} uses a separate @code{R-001} through @code{R-118} series;
those identifiers are reserved and are not assumed to map one-to-one to the
new items. The implementation baseline shall include a crosswalk for any
older R item carried forward, revised or retired; no old identifier is
reused. All items in this revision are initially unchecked.

Research date: @value{UPDATED}. Local policy baseline: The Steelbore
Standard v2.13, read from its canonical Texinfo masthead. A later baseline
must be verified before adoption. Product posture is Personal / Hobby.
Game registration, assurance classification and the public project URL are
implementation prerequisites, not approvals asserted by this document.

@node Product
@chapter Product

Tharsis shall reimplement the original 1996 Quake engine in Rust, preserving
its gameplay while using modern GPU execution for rendering. The result is
an independently maintained engine for user-supplied content, not a remake
of the campaign or a port of Quake II, Quake III, or the 2021 rerelease engine.

@table @strong
@item Player need
Install a small engine, select an existing Quake data directory, play the
original campaign, save progress, and obtain consistent frame pacing on
modern Linux hardware. Classic visuals remain available.
@item Maintainer need
Own a comprehensible Rust engine with bounded input handling, repeatable
compatibility tests, and measured GPU improvements. Rust GPU is exercised
in a real game, with its compiler risks made explicit.
@item Mod author need
Load vanilla-format maps and QuakeC programs, receive useful errors, and
understand the exact supported limits and extensions.
@end table

@strong{MVP experience.} A player launches Tharsis, selects a read-only
@file{id1} directory, chooses a difficulty, completes a campaign, saves and
reloads, and switches between Classic and Enhanced rendering without
changing physics. Missing data opens an explanation and directory selector;
the application does not download game assets. An unsupported GPU offers
software presentation or a useful diagnostic.

The MVP supports Linux x86-64 on Wayland and X11, with Vulkan as the primary
GPU backend and a software presentation path. Windows/DX12 and macOS/Metal
are M7 validation targets, not promises inferred from wgpu portability.
Browser WebGPU, mobile, consoles and VR are outside the MVP. OpenGL is not
an MVP GPU backend: a compute-dependent renderer cannot assume every GL
adapter exposes its required capabilities.

@table @strong
@item Required content compatibility
User-supplied original shareware episode and registered @file{id1} campaign;
PAK, BSP29, MDL, SPR, WAD2, LMP, WAV and QuakeC v6; the qualified mod corpus;
protocol-15 demo playback and recording; documented vanilla save support.
@item Free acceptance content
A pinned, license-audited LibreQuake subset and project-owned fixtures for
public CI. The subset must use formats and limits supported by the MVP.
LibreQuake is not assumed to contain id's demos or reproduce id's campaign.
@item Deferred
Mission packs, BSP2/2PSB, FitzQuake protocol 666, external replacement
textures, rerelease-specific extensions, UDP multiplayer and QuakeWorld.
QuakeWorld requires a separate compatibility decision, beyond M10 NetQuake.
@item Excluded
Bundled proprietary game data, matchmaking services, telemetry, a new
general-purpose game engine, mandatory ray tracing, and a requirement for
bit-identical simulation across arbitrary compilers and CPU architectures.
@end table

The shipped engine's project-owned CPU implementation shall be Rust.
Platform libraries, graphics drivers and audited dependencies may contain
native code; the product shall not claim the entire process is pure Rust.
The original C engine is a pinned, test-only reference outside the shipped
runtime. QuakeC remains the content language; rewriting mods in Rust is not
required.

@strong{Compatibility boundary.} M0 shall create a versioned compatibility
manifest identifying the accepted original shareware and registered data
editions by release, distribution provenance and SHA-256 hashes of their
input PAK files. It shall also identify the qualified LibreQuake release,
project-owned fixture revision and a named set of vanilla-format mods with
versions, hashes and licensing. The initial set shall exercise custom maps,
QuakeC, assets, saves and demo playback. M0 shall record any still-unnamed
candidate as unqualified; a release shall not claim compatibility for it.

For each claimed corpus entry the manifest shall document accepted engine,
format, entity, model, file, VM and memory limits, commands and builtins;
the test, platform, input hash, result and known deviations; and whether its
input and outputs can be used in public CI. The initial VM instruction budget
is 100,000 per invocation, configurable as described under Quality. Project
owners shall set and test the remaining numeric limits in M0 before a claim is
baselined. Sandboxed file access, restricted commands and configured resource
budgets may exclude otherwise vanilla mods; document failures with a reason.
Adding a compatibility claim requires a pinned, licensed corpus entry and a
passing regression test. Public CI uses only approved redistributable fixtures;
licensed original data and results that contain protected material stay in
the authorized local acceptance environment.

@node Technology
@chapter Technology

The following are researched observations, followed by product decisions.
Version numbers are a dated starting point; M0 freezes an exact toolchain
and lockfile after a working spike.

@table @strong
@item wgpu 30.0.1 — selected host graphics API
The upstream release index and API documentation list 30.0.1 at research
time. wgpu provides Rust graphics and compute abstractions over multiple
native APIs. Use its validated path for the production baseline. Adoption
does not imply identical feature support on every backend. Sources S1–S3.
@item Rust GPU and cargo-gpu — selected shader authoring direction
Rust GPU compiles a supported Rust subset to SPIR-V. Its README still warns
that it is not production-ready. cargo-gpu has moved into the Rust GPU
repository. Pin both compiler and shader dependencies; isolate their
required toolchain from the stable host build. Sources S4–S5.
@item Naga and WGSL — portability and contingency
Use Naga validation/translation where the pinned implementation supports the
generated shader. Rust-to-SPIR-V-to-WGSL is a hypothesis to prove for each
entry point, not an automatic guarantee. Retain a small WGSL comparison
implementation during the spike. A production WGSL substitution requires a
recorded change to the Rust-shader product requirement. Source S3.
@item Bevy — evaluated alternative
Bevy provides a broader engine and evolving rendering facilities, including
Solari work. Choose a focused wgpu engine here because Quake's entity layout,
VM and execution order are compatibility constraints. This is an
architecture judgment, not a claim that Bevy cannot implement Quake. S6.
@item CubeCL — later compute experiment
CubeCL offers Rust-authored compute across several backends. Evaluate it
only for measured preprocessing or compute workloads; do not introduce a
second GPU runtime merely to render Quake. S7.
@item Rust CUDA — not a runtime dependency
Its CUDA-oriented execution model does not meet this project's cross-vendor
baseline. It may inform isolated research, but shall not be required to
play, build, package or validate the MVP. S8.
@end table

@strong{Latest-technology policy.} Shipping uses pinned releases and proven
capabilities. Experimental APIs live behind build-time and runtime gates.
Upgrades require replaying the shader, compatibility and benchmark corpora.
No dependency follows an unpinned development branch in a release.

@strong{M0 stop/go test.} Target an initial two-engineer-week spike; duration
is an estimate, not an acceptance substitute. Before running it, name one
integrated and one discrete Linux Vulkan reference adapter, OS and driver,
and record the source fixture, shader toolchain and output hashes. Both
adapters shall compile the pinned Rust vertex, fragment and compute shaders,
pass wgpu validation and buffer-layout tests, draw a textured and lightmapped
BSP fixture, execute an indirect draw, and pass the predeclared CPU/GPU
sample and image comparisons. Freeze the sample vectors, numeric tolerances,
image threshold, resolution and expected output before the test. Run each
test on both adapters and publish a result and capability matrix. Test any
claimed secondary backend separately; the Linux MVP does not depend on it.

Q-003 passes only when all required evidence passes on both named adapters
and the source rebuild under Q-002 succeeds. An unsuccessful spike is a
failed gate, even if the failure is documented: M0 and the Rust-shader MVP
remain blocked. The maintainer may approve a written PRD scope change,
including a narrower hardware commitment or WGSL substitution, or defer the
release; then rebaseline and rerun the revised gate. Do not silently replace
the shader language or claim an untested adapter.

@strong{Shader source and packaging contract.} The source tree contains
Rust shader source, reviewed generated SPIR-V, a manifest of output hashes,
dependency locks and exact host, shader compiler, translator and backend
versions. Ordinary application builds use the generated artifacts and a
pinned stable host toolchain; they shall not download or install nightly
Rust or regenerate shaders. The shader regeneration job uses the separately
pinned Rust GPU toolchain and its required nightly compiler (S5, S12).

For regeneration, obtain the declared source inputs, compiler artifacts and
build dependencies through an explicitly documented, checksum-verified
provisioning step. With those inputs preloaded, a clean isolated build shall
run with network disabled, regenerate all shipped shader artifacts, compare
their bytes and hashes to the manifest, and validate each entry point through
the pinned wgpu/Naga path. A mismatch or validation failure blocks release.
The packaging build uses the same reviewed artifacts and lockfiles; release
CI records their provenance and independently repeats regeneration. If
byte reproducibility is unattainable, it blocks this requirement until a
documented deterministic normalization and scope change are approved.

@node Architecture
@chapter Architecture

Port gameplay-sensitive algorithms incrementally from the GPL reference,
replacing global variables and pointers with owned state, checked indices
and explicit lifetimes. Preserve edict field offsets, allocation order,
touch ordering, builtin semantics and numeric conversion behavior where
observable. Avoid a wholesale ECS rewrite during the compatibility phase.

@table @code
@item tharsis-data / tharsis-fs
Read-only content search, checked format decoders and immutable map data.
Reject traversal and malformed ranges before allocation or dereference.
@item tharsis-qc / tharsis-sim
QuakeC interpreter, edicts, hull collision, movement, AI, triggers and level
progression. These crates forbid unsafe Rust and have no renderer dependency.
@item tharsis-protocol / tharsis-client
Protocol-15 codecs, local transport, demo reader, client interpolation and
presentation state. MVP transport is in-process; no listener is opened.
@item tharsis-shade / tharsis-shaders
CPU-testable no_std shading functions and separate GPU entry points.
Share suitable math and semantics, not assumptions about identical CPU/GPU
floating-point results. Validate alignment, strides and binding layouts.
@item tharsis-render / tharsis-soft
wgpu renderer and software reference/presentation renderer consuming the
same immutable render snapshot. Only the graphics boundary owns GPU objects.
@item tharsis-platform / tharsis-audio / tharsis-app
Window and input handling, audio devices, settings, menus and lifecycle.
Use winit/cpal or equivalents only after dependency and license review.
@item tharsis-tools
Headless asset inspection, scripted simulation, demo playback, trace
comparison and machine-readable benchmark reports.
@end table

Simulation executes serially in authoritative order. QuakeC and collision
callbacks can mutate the same world; parallelizing those operations risks
behavioral changes and is unlikely to help this workload before profiling.
This is the explicit serial trade-off required by Standard §3.2.

Rendering overlaps simulation using immutable snapshots and bounded queues.
The window event loop remains on the platform-required thread. Asset
decoding and independent test cases may use a bounded worker pool. Parallel
command encoding is adopted only if total frame time improves. GPU compute
passes do not imply separate hardware queues or guaranteed asynchronous
compute overlap.

Reliable gameplay commands shall never be dropped. A full reliable queue
uses bounded backpressure at a safe boundary or a reported session error.
Old visual snapshots may be replaced by the newest complete snapshot.
Audio overload may coalesce disposable one-shot effects; stop, volume and
channel-state updates must remain consistent so sounds cannot get stuck.
The audio callback performs no file I/O, blocking locks or allocation.

Normal play initially uses a proposed 72 Hz simulation step with render
interpolation. This is a product setting, not a claim that every original
Quake mode used that fixed rate. A compatibility harness feeds the same
ordered commands, random seed and explicit timestep sequence to both
implementations. Cap catch-up work; pause local play on suspend and reset
the interpolation clock on resume. Slow rendering shall not change the
simulation step. Demos exercise client messages, not authoritative server
physics; server parity requires the separate command-driven harness. S10.

The compatibility contract is the pinned original engine driven by the
same explicit timestep sequence and ordered input events, not an assertion
that the original ran at 72 Hz by default. M0 freezes a corpus containing a
constant 1/72-second sequence and representative variable-step sequences;
the variable sequences test the headless compatibility harness and do not
change the fixed-step player mode. For each case record the float encoding,
reference build, seed, input times and per-field tolerances before comparing.
Match discrete event order, trigger activation and map transitions exactly;
compare movement positions/velocities and weapon event times using those
frozen limits, including boundary cases around the next simulation tick.
Demo compatibility separately checks message decode, rendered client states
and recording/replay at the declared demo timing. Any observable default
fixed-step divergence from the qualified reference requires a documented,
maintainer-approved exception and a regression fixture before release; a
known difference is not counted as a passing parity test.

@node Rendering
@chapter Rendering

@table @strong
@item Tier 0 — software resilience
CPU rasterization at a conservative internal resolution, with a window
presentation path independent of a working wgpu device. Headless reference
rendering is also available. This tier preserves playability, not the GPU
performance target.
@item Tier 1 — portable raster baseline
Indexed triangle rendering, CPU BSP/PVS and frustum visibility, ordinary
texture bindings, GPU light-style blending, GPU alias-model transforms and
shader water/sky. No bindless, subgroups, timestamp queries or ray tracing
is required. This is the correctness baseline for all optimized paths.
@item Tier 2 — GPU-driven rendering
Compute visibility, bounded indirect command buffers, batched materials,
optional texture binding arrays, optional subgroups and pipeline caching.
The implementation and correctness tests are required for M4. Automatic
selection is separately conditional on supported capabilities and the
performance adoption gate; keep Tier 1 as the default where Tier 2 fails it.
An explicit experimental selection may be offered with its status disclosed.
@item Tier 3 — experimental rendering
Mesh shaders, ray queries, ray-tracing pipelines and path tracing are
separately selectable experiments. The wgpu 30.0.1 documentation marks
these APIs experimental. Ray queries are documented as native Vulkan;
mesh-shader translation has backend restrictions, including Naga support
limited to Vulkan. A hardware feature bit alone does not prove shader
compiler support. S3.
@end table

Each frame extracts a render snapshot, selects the camera leaf/PVS, culls
eligible surfaces, draws opaque world and models, draws ordered transparent
effects, applies the selected color transform, draws the HUD, and presents.
Render dependencies and GPU resource transitions are explicit. Reuse bounded
transient buffers and avoid CPU readback in the visibility path.

Static BSP geometry, source lightmaps and model frames are uploaded at map
load and retained until eviction or device recovery. Dynamic data includes
entity transforms, camera/PVS information, animated style values and lights;
moving brush models remain dynamic instances. Texture changes and cache
eviction invalidate only affected resources.

The lightmap shader combines the per-surface style layers. Dynamic-light
evaluation must reproduce the declared Classic lighting equation and be
compared with reference samples. Enhanced lighting can differ visibly but
must never affect hit detection, AI visibility or gameplay state. Do not
promise that applying a similar falloff formula alone reproduces the
software surface cache and palette lookup.

Compute culling intersects PVS membership and frustum tests; absent or invalid
visibility data falls back to a conservative visible set. Moving doors and
teleports must not produce false occlusion. Optional hierarchical-depth
occlusion needs separate conservative tests and is deferred until profiling.
Cap indirect writes and validate overflow counters. Before dispatch, compare
a conservative upper bound on candidate commands to buffer capacity; if it
does not fit, render the complete frame through Tier 1. A supposedly
impossible GPU-side overflow shall suppress the incomplete result and use a
complete conservative draw in the same frame before presentation; a
GPU-controlled fallback or exceptional synchronization may implement this.
Never present partially culled geometry as a successful frame. Inject both
preflight and unexpected-overflow cases and compare visible surface coverage
against Tier 1; report overflow and retain bounded memory use.

If indirect-count drawing is unsupported, use a bounded fixed command list
with zero-count entries when supported, or ordinary indexed draws. If
texture arrays/non-uniform indexing are unsupported, batch by material.
Atlases require mip gutters and repeat-aware coordinates to avoid seams;
they are an optimization, not a mandatory universal fallback. Never make
the baseline depend on one CPU draw call per pass.

Classic mode uses nearest texture sampling, original palette/colormap
behavior, documented fullbright handling, sky, water, sprites, particles,
view blends and an optional low-resolution presentation. Model interpolation
is independently switchable. Enhanced mode adds linear filtering, mipmaps,
anisotropy where supported, improved interpolation and selected antialiasing.
Both modes expose their settings in benchmark records.

HDR is post-MVP. A floating-point render target alone is not HDR output:
presentation also needs supported display/surface color space, transfer
function, luminance policy and an SDR fallback. Shader/pipeline compilation
is warmed before timed play where practical. Disk caches are optional,
bounded, keyed by adapter, driver, shader hash and build version, and safe
to discard. Bad cache data must not prevent startup.

@node Quality
@chapter Quality and acceptance policy

@strong{Fidelity.} Use the original engine at a pinned commit as a behavioral
oracle, with its build flags, CPU, content hashes and test harness changes
recorded. Exact entity ordering, discrete state, opcodes and protocol bytes
must match where specified. Numeric comparisons use declared per-field
tolerances and event checks. Exact floating-point equivalence may be required
within a qualified reference environment, but is not inferred from source
transliteration or a ban on fused operations.

The acceptance corpus covers stairs, slopes, wall sliding, water, lifts,
doors, teleporters, trigger order, damage, weapon timing, monster AI, secrets,
map transitions, intermissions and endings. It includes playthrough evidence
for every supported registered map on a machine with authorized data.
Public CI uses freely redistributable fixtures; private runs emit sanitized
results without publishing game assets or derived reference images by default.

Software reference frames use exact palette-index checks for qualified
deterministic scenes. M0 shall name fixture scenes for opaque geometry,
light styles, dynamic lights, water/sky, sprites/models, particles and HUD,
including source data hashes and camera/time/input state. Fix resolution,
viewport, color space, gamma, filtering, blend and interpolation settings for
each capture. M3 shall freeze software reference images generated from the
pinned original and establish exact checks for the qualified deterministic
subset. M4 shall compare Classic GPU captures against corresponding software
references where the pipelines represent the same scene; separately approved
GPU golden images cover intentional rasterization differences and Enhanced
mode. Each GPU golden records its reference adapter and driver, generation
method, rationale and relationship to the software image.

For the first M4 comparison, use SSIM at least 0.99 and at most 0.5 percent
of pixels exceeding 8/255 channel error as proposed starting thresholds, not
an already validated claim. After the first working renderer, measure the
corpus on the reference adapters and propose per-scene thresholds and masks.
The maintainer shall approve and freeze them with evidence before they gate
changes; UI text and critical visibility retain exact region/geometry checks
and human review. Threshold or mask changes require a dated explanation,
before/after captures and maintainer approval. Private content-derived images
remain in the authorized environment unless redistribution is verified.

@strong{Proposed resource targets.} These are engineering targets, not
measured results. M0 selects named available machines and freezes exact
CPU, GPU, RAM, OS, driver, power mode and display settings. Until then no
performance compliance claim is valid.

@multitable @columnfractions .25 .35 .40
@headitem Profile @tab Workload @tab Target
@item Integrated GPU
@tab 1920×1080, Tier 1 Classic, approved original-map corpus
@tab Median frame interval at most 8.33 ms; p99 at most 16.67 ms
@item Discrete GPU
@tab 2560×1440, forced Tier 2 Enhanced, native resolution, no ray tracing
@tab Median at most 4.17 ms; p99 at most 8.33 ms
@item Simulation
@tab Busiest approved vanilla fixture on each reference CPU
@tab p99 step time at most 1 ms
@item Map loading
@tab Largest approved original map, local SSD, warm filesystem cache
@tab At most 1 s; report cold-load time separately
@item Resident memory
@tab Original content, no replacement assets, steady play
@tab Host RSS at most 512 MiB; GPU resources at most 512 MiB
@end multitable

Measure at least five 120-second runs after a 30-second warmup, excluding
loading from steady-frame metrics but reporting it separately. Record
median, p95, p99 and worst frame interval, CPU time, available GPU timestamps,
allocations and dropped/deferred work. Measure uncapped throughput with
vsync disabled separately from paced presentation. Timestamp unavailability
is reported as unavailable, never as zero GPU cost. Shared CI runners are
correctness gates; designated hardware provides performance evidence.

Implement Tier 2 for M4, including a forceable test path, regardless of its
measured speed. Enable it automatically for an adapter/workload class only if
it preserves the correctness corpus, improves relevant median frame time by
at least 5 percent beyond observed run variance, and does not worsen p99 by
more than 2 percent or exceed resource targets. If it fails, use Tier 1 by
default and record the result; failing to win does not block completion of
the Tier 2 implementation. Apply the same enablement gate to other optional
optimizations, with a justified exception for a visual-quality feature.
Before release the maintainer approves the named machine and workload matrix,
targets and thresholds. Any target revision requires a dated justification,
old and proposed values, measured results and maintainer approval; report
both original and revised targets in the release evidence. Small vanilla
maps may favor CPU culling; automatic tier selection must respect that result.
Measure input-to-present latency separately; the initial target is no more
than one display interval above Tier 1 under matched presentation settings.

@strong{Untrusted input.} Bound files, decoded counts, recursion, VM
instructions, config expansion and allocations. Validate integer arithmetic,
indices and ranges before use. Start with a configurable 100,000-instruction
budget per QuakeC invocation; record compatibility exceptions. An error
abandons the affected load/session and returns a diagnostic. Never resume
partially executed world mutation as though the VM call succeeded.

Content mounts are read-only. Saves, configs, logs and captures use separate
user-owned writable roots. Reject absolute paths, traversal and symlink
escapes; command execution from mods cannot launch a shell or arbitrary
process. Native plugins and untrusted shader replacements are outside MVP.
Fuzz loaders, VM, demos, saves and config commands; release requires no
unresolved reproducible crash in the maintained regression corpus.

On GPU loss, preserve CPU state, pause local play, attempt one bounded
renderer rebuild and reupload, then offer software mode or save-and-exit.
If the whole graphics process/driver fails, recovery is best effort; no
claim is made that every GPU hang is recoverable. Audio loss falls back to
silent play. Writes use temporary files and atomic replacement where
supported; simulated failed writes must preserve the last valid save.

@strong{Licensing and identity.} The upstream engine source includes a
GPL-2.0-or-later grant; proposed new engine code uses GPL-3.0-or-later,
preserving upstream notices and per-file provenance. This document uses
CC-BY-SA-4.0. Game data has separate terms: the source release explicitly
retains data restrictions, including on the demo/shareware distribution.
Do not bundle, mirror or publicly cache id assets without verifying the
applicable permission. Audit the specific LibreQuake release and included
files before CI redistribution. Sources S9–S11.

Use Tharsis branding and describe Quake compatibility without implying
endorsement. Credit id Software and substantially used third-party work in
CREDITS.md. Local project initialization must register the name and game
classification through the standard's normal branch/PR process; the checked
v2.13 registry does not yet list Tharsis. Until registration, do not assert
the games exemption or Category D as already granted. Reassess assurance
and licensing when adding network service behavior.

Regardless of exemption, require remappable controls, scalable HUD/text,
independent audio levels, a reduced-flash option and no color-only error
messages. New launcher/tooling surfaces must meet their applicable
accessibility requirements. Original game art keeps its original palette;
new product chrome uses the named steelbore theme and licensed fonts.

@node M0
@chapter M0 — Foundation and shader feasibility

@strong{Exit:} every required task passes, including a reproducible
Rust-shader vertical slice on both reference adapters. A decision to stop or
change scope records the failure but does not satisfy the existing gate.
Estimate: 2–4 engineer-weeks. Five required tasks.
@itemize @bullet
@item [ ] R-201 (Q-001) Declare posture, proposed assurance, license provenance, game registration and authoritative AGENTS.md; record the historical PRD identifier crosswalk and inspect against the verified Standard version
@item [ ] R-202 (Q-002) Pin and provision host and shader toolchains, inputs and generated artifacts; verify stable-host offline build, isolated offline shader regeneration, byte hashes and wgpu/Naga validation
@item [ ] R-203 (Q-003) Pass every documented vertex, fragment, compute, indirect-draw and output comparison on both named Linux adapters; failure blocks M0 pending an approved rebaseline
@item [ ] R-204 (Q-004) Freeze the licensed content/mod manifest, limits and reference engine; specify fixed/variable timestep inputs and per-field/event comparisons, with a demonstrable failing mismatch
@item [ ] R-205 (Q-005) Record named integrated/discrete reference machines, capability matrix, fixture and capture settings, baseline timings and maintainer-approved performance targets
@end itemize

@node M1
@chapter M1 — Resources and virtual machine

@strong{Exit:} assets and QuakeC load safely; opcode behavior matches the
reference corpus. Estimate: 4–7 engineer-weeks. Five required tasks.
@itemize @bullet
@item [ ] R-206 (Q-006) Parse PAK/BSP29/WAD2/MDL/SPR/LMP/WAV and validate malformed and truncated fixtures with bounded allocation
@item [ ] R-207 (Q-007) Implement documented content precedence, read-only mounts and confined writable paths; test traversal and symlink escapes
@item [ ] R-208 (Q-008) Execute all vanilla QuakeC v6 opcodes and builtins with checked indices, edict layout and reference-result tests
@item [ ] R-209 (Q-009) Bound VM execution and config expansion; abort invalid sessions safely and test infinite loops and prohibited commands
@item [ ] R-210 (Q-010) Add headless inspection and fuzz targets with a minimized permanent regression corpus
@end itemize

@node M2
@chapter M2 — Simulation and gameplay

@strong{Exit:} deterministic command-driven gameplay traces pass the
qualified oracle checks. Estimate: 6–10 engineer-weeks. Five required tasks.
@itemize @bullet
@item [ ] R-211 (Q-011) Port hull collision, area linking and touch order; pass stairs, slopes, water, lifts, doors and teleport fixtures
@item [ ] R-212 (Q-012) Implement player/monster physics, weapons, damage, pickups and triggers; compare state and event timing to the oracle
@item [ ] R-213 (Q-013) Implement spawning, map transitions, difficulty, secrets, intermissions and endings; verify all supported progression paths
@item [ ] R-214 (Q-014) Decouple simulation and rendering with bounded transport; test overload, pause, suspend and resume without command loss
