@node Delivery
@chapter Delivery and decision record

The critical dependency path is M0, M1, M2, M3, M4, M5, M6. Rendering can
start against synthetic snapshots after M0; audio can start after resource
loading. Integration and oracle validation remain on the critical path.
The sum is approximately 28–49 engineer-weeks for one experienced Rust/graphics
developer with part-time test support; this is a planning estimate with high
uncertainty, not a calendar commitment. Re-estimate after M0 and the first
playable M3 map. Do not convert parallelizable tasks into guaranteed schedule
savings. M7–M10 require separate estimates.

@table @strong
@item Compiler/translation risk — high
Rust GPU may fail needed language features or backend translations. Owner:
renderer maintainer. Resolve in M0; preserve the stable host/toolchain split,
reproducible shader source build and explicit WGSL decision path.
@item Compatibility drift — high
Timing, float conversions and entity order can subtly change gameplay.
Owner: simulation maintainer. Use the command-driven oracle from M0 onward;
demo playback alone cannot discharge server requirements.
@item Optimization overhead — medium
Dispatches and synchronization can cost more than CPU work on small maps.
Owner: renderer maintainer. Keep Tier 1 and use the measured adoption gate.
@item Asset/test availability — high
Registered content cannot be assumed available to public CI. Owner: release
maintainer. Maintain free fixtures and a private authorized playthrough gate.
@item Unsafe dependency/driver boundary — medium
Safe project Rust does not eliminate dependency bugs or GPU hangs. Owner:
platform maintainer. Audit dependencies, isolate unsafe interfaces, retain
diagnostics and test failure paths without claiming total fault immunity.
@item Scope growth — high
Software-renderer parity, mods and network support can dominate effort.
Owner: product maintainer. Protect M0–M6 and require scope decisions for
new formats, protocols or mandatory experimental graphics.
@end table

Open decisions are intentionally narrow: exact reference machines; the
qualified LibreQuake release/subset; accepted numeric/image tolerances after
the spike; shader fallback authorization if M0 fails; and final game/name
registration. The selected defaults are Linux-first, original Quake,
Rust-authored shaders, no network in MVP, and optional experimental graphics.
None of these decisions prevents writing or reviewing this PRD.

For each Q requirement, the implementation record shall name an owner,
verification artifact and pass/fail status. Mark a task complete only after
its exit evidence exists. CI should cover formatting, linting, unit and
integration tests, shader validation/regeneration, dependency/license audits
and fuzz regressions; dedicated hardware runs the graphics/performance
matrix. Record policy tailoring proportional to the finalized assurance
category. No full Standard conformance claim is made by this draft.

